  "rationale": "structural: The subject text is: \"The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to take every change to agreed requirement text, including a rewording for precision and the removal of a requirement, through the same review and agreement before committing it.\"\n\nThis clause is about what the skill document tells agents to do. It names no library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or delegation through a named interface.\n\nThe only candidate structural clause is that the guidance belongs to the `habeas-requirements` skill. That is satisfied by the root `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, whose front matter has `name: habeas-requirements`. It is not a real structural constraint, though. Any other layout of the same guidance, such as a different section order or wording, would meet the clause equally well, so the layout is discretionary.\n\nThe substantive obligation is the skill's content: that it covers rewordings and removals as well as new text, and that it requires the same review and agreement before commit. That content is observable behavior of the document. The behavioral and test criteria should assess it, not this structural one.\n\nI did not inspect the body sections `section[Habeas%20requirements]` and `section@7843029c802e`, because no structural obligation depends on them. No implementation evidence is needed here, so Success is appropriate.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has one observable obligation. The `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to send every change to agreed requirement text through the same review and agreement before committing it. That includes a rewording for precision and the removal of a requirement.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reviews_every_change_to_agreed_text]` is marked `{test}` for it. It checks the bundled skill text in three ways:\n- `assert_phrases` requires the bundled skill to contain \"Every change to agreed text, including a rewording for precision or 